---
import set from 'virtual:icon-set'
import Example from '@chassis-ui/docs/shortcodes/Example.astro'
import { fontIcon, spriteIcon, svgPath } from '@libs/icon'

const name = set.exampleIcon
---

<section id="usage-section" class="home-section py-3xl md:py-6xl">
  <div class="container 2xl section-content">
    <div class="row mb-3xl md:mb-6xl">
      <header class="section-header col lg:col-10 xl:col-8">
        <h2 id="usage">Usage</h2>
        <p>
          The build writes the set in three forms: an SVG sprite, one SVG file per icon and an icon
          font. The examples show <code>{name}</code>, and the page of each icon has the code for
          that icon. On this site <a href="https://chassis-ui.com/css">Chassis CSS</a> sizes and colors
          an element with the class <code>icon</code>.
        </p>
      </header>
    </div>

    <div class="row my-lg mt-4xl">
      <div class="md:col-4">
        <h3>Sprite</h3>
        <p>
          The sprite holds every icon as a <code>&lt;symbol&gt;</code> whose <code>id</code> is the name
          of the icon. One request loads the set, a <code>&lt;use&gt;</code> element draws an icon, and
          the icon takes the color of the text around it.
        </p>
        <p>
          A <code>&lt;use&gt;</code> element does not load a file from another origin, so serve the sprite
          from the origin of the page.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="md:col-8">
        <Example code={spriteIcon(name)} />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row my-lg">
      <div class="md:col-4">
        <h3>External image</h3>
        <p>
          The SVG file of an icon is an image for the <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> element. An image does
          not take the color of the text: the icon is black.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="md:col-8">
        <Example
          code={`<img src="${svgPath(name)}" alt="" width="${set.frame}" height="${set.frame}">`}
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row my-lg">
      <div class="md:col-4">
        <h3>Icon font</h3>
        <p>
          The stylesheet of the font has one class per icon, <code>{set.prefix}-&lt;name&gt;</code>.
          Load the stylesheet, and put the class on an empty element.
        </p>
        <p>
          Use <code>font-size</code> and <code>color</code> to change the size and the color of the icon.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="md:col-8">
        <Example code={fontIcon(name)} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
